00:02:15,020 --> 00:02:22,430
So adding it in and now clicking play you will see that now when we touch the ladybug if I can touch

43
00:02:22,430 --> 00:02:23,150
it.

44
00:02:23,330 --> 00:02:24,380
There you go.

45
00:02:24,470 --> 00:02:27,950
But notice how suddenly I can no longer control the dragonfly.

46
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:29,720
It just like freezes.

47
00:02:30,020 --> 00:02:31,040
Why is this?

48
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:35,670
Well, if we look at our script logically, taking the code out again.

49
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,120
Look, this is all a loop, so we are constantly checking if any of these conditions are true and if

50
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:43,200
one of them is true.

51
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,720
Like we click the down arrow, it goes down.

52
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:49,260
But remember how I said code runs from the top down?

53
00:02:49,470 --> 00:02:50,940
Well, that is happening here.

54
00:02:51,030 --> 00:02:55,530
So when we click start it's going to check if the right arrow has been pressed.

55
00:02:55,530 --> 00:02:59,550
Then it's going to check if the left arrow is being pressed, then the up then the down.

56
00:02:59,550 --> 00:03:03,990
So it's not really checking all these conditions at the same time.

57
00:03:03,990 --> 00:03:05,970
It's going from the top down.

58
00:03:05,970 --> 00:03:10,380
But because it happened so fast, it seems like it's checking it all at the same time.

59
00:03:10,380 --> 00:03:18,120
However, if I were to add a wait one second like to this, check for the down arrow.

60
00:03:18,420 --> 00:03:23,490
Notice how our other arrows work perfectly, but as soon as it checks for the down arrow, all the other

61
00:03:23,490 --> 00:03:24,510
arrows freeze up.

62
00:03:25,370 --> 00:03:29,000
And this is because it's running the code from the top down.

63
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,700
And when it gets to this code, it needs to wait for a second.

64
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:35,600
And it can only continue the loop once the second is over.

65
00:03:37,030 --> 00:03:41,170
So basically that is why this code has the same problem.

66
00:03:41,170 --> 00:03:48,160
Because there is this say yum for two seconds and it will only continue looping the code once it is

67
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:49,960
finished executing the script.

68
00:03:50,290 --> 00:03:55,270
So that is one of the reasons we use another script, so we can have multiple things running at the

69
00:03:55,270 --> 00:03:57,010
same time without any issue.

70
00:03:57,490 --> 00:04:02,470
Now I could take out this, say yum for two seconds and add it back into the loop.

71
00:04:02,470 --> 00:04:06,580
Look, if we play it again, you'll see it works and it doesn't freeze.

72
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:13,630
But even if you just wanted our sprite to play a sound, you should still use a separate script for

73
00:04:13,630 --> 00:04:14,170
this.

74
00:04:14,170 --> 00:04:20,020
Basically, having separate scripts is just so much better for keeping things organized.

75
00:04:20,050 --> 00:04:26,110
You see, when you make really big programs, really big gains, things can get quite confusing.

76
00:04:26,110 --> 00:04:32,200
And if you have just one ultra mega long script, you might not know what's going on.

77
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:38,470
So what we suggest is that each script you have should do just one thing.

78
00:04:38,470 --> 00:04:41,470
Remember that each script should just do one thing.

79
00:04:41,470 --> 00:04:43,480
This script controls the arrow keys.

80
00:04:43,480 --> 00:04:48,430
This one checks if we're touching the ladybug, and if you are well organized like this from the start,

81
00:04:48,430 --> 00:04:55,030
you'll be really thankful in the future when you make some fun and really complex games.

82
00:04:55,030 --> 00:05:00,850
So with that, there's just one more thing to code for our game to be complete, and that is getting

83
